# Battery and charging

## Overview

Linux describes every power source under `/sys/class/power_supply`, one
directory per supply and one small text file per value. The phone has
three:

| Supply | What it is |
|---|---|
| `qg-battery` | The battery's fuel gauge: level, voltage, current, temperature |
| `pmi632-charger` | The charger in the PMI632 power chip |
| `tcpm-source-psy-…` | The USB-C port |

```elixir
File.ls!("/sys/class/power_supply")
```

## Reading a value

Each value is a file. This helper reads one and turns numbers into
integers:

```elixir
read = fn supply, name ->
  [dir] = Path.wildcard("/sys/class/power_supply/#{supply}*")

  case File.read(Path.join(dir, name)) do
    {:ok, text} ->
      text = String.trim(text)

      case Integer.parse(text) do
        {number, ""} -> number
        _ -> text
      end

    {:error, reason} ->
      {:error, reason}
  end
end

read.("qg-battery", "capacity")
```

## The battery at a glance

The kernel reports voltages in microvolts (µV), currents in microamps
(µA) and the temperature in tenths of a degree.

```elixir
%{
  level: "#{read.("qg-battery", "capacity")} %",
  status: read.("qg-battery", "status"),
  health: read.("qg-battery", "health"),
  voltage: "#{Float.round(read.("qg-battery", "voltage_now") / 1_000_000, 2)} V",
  current: "#{div(read.("qg-battery", "current_now"), 1000)} mA",
  temperature: "#{read.("qg-battery", "temp") / 10} °C",
  technology: read.("qg-battery", "technology")
}
```

`status` is `"Charging"`, `"Discharging"`, `"Not charging"` or `"Full"`.

## Is it plugged in?

```elixir
%{
  cable_plugged_in: read.("pmi632-charger", "online") == 1,
  charger_status: read.("pmi632-charger", "status"),
  charger_input: "#{Float.round(read.("pmi632-charger", "voltage_now") / 1_000_000, 2)} V",
  usb_c_mode: read.("tcpm-source-psy", "usb_type")
}
```

In `usb_c_mode` the value in brackets is the one in use.

## How long will it last?

The battery was designed to hold 3000 mAh. The level tells how much of
that is left, and the average current tells how fast it is going in or
out. Dividing one by the other gives a rough time.

```elixir
design_mah = div(read.("qg-battery", "charge_full_design"), 1000)
level = read.("qg-battery", "capacity")
current_ma = abs(div(read.("qg-battery", "current_avg"), 1000))
status = read.("qg-battery", "status")

left_mah = design_mah * level / 100

hours =
  cond do
    current_ma == 0 -> nil
    status == "Charging" -> (design_mah - left_mah) / current_ma
    true -> left_mah / current_ma
  end

case {status, hours} do
  {_, nil} -> "No current flowing right now."
  {"Charging", h} -> "About #{Float.round(h, 1)} hours to full."
  {_, h} -> "About #{Float.round(h, 1)} hours left at the current drain."
end
```

This is an estimate: an ageing battery holds less than its design
capacity, and the charger slows down as the battery gets full.

## Live view

Plug and unplug the USB cable while this runs for thirty seconds.

```elixir
frame = Kino.Frame.new() |> Kino.render()

# 30 rounds; each reads the battery, then waits one second.
for _round <- 1..30 do
  text =
    "#{read.("qg-battery", "capacity")} %   " <>
      "#{read.("qg-battery", "status")}   " <>
      "#{div(read.("qg-battery", "current_now"), 1000)} mA   " <>
      "#{Float.round(read.("qg-battery", "voltage_now") / 1_000_000, 2)} V"

  Kino.Frame.render(frame, Kino.Markdown.new("`#{text}`"))
  Process.sleep(1000)
end

:done
```

## Everything the kernel reports

```elixir
dir = hd(Path.wildcard("/sys/class/power_supply/qg-battery"))

for name <- Enum.sort(File.ls!(dir)), File.regular?(Path.join(dir, name)), name != "uevent" do
  {name, read.("qg-battery", name)}
end
```

Try the same with `"pmi632-charger"`.
